  <h2 id="failures">When things fail</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>A step that raises</strong> is retried up to its <code>retries</code>, then
    fails. The steps downstream of it are skipped, while other partitions' steps that
    don't depend on it carry on, so one bad month doesn't stop the others; the run ends
    failed and can be resumed. <strong>A process that dies</strong> leaves its run
    running with a stale heartbeat; <code>resume</code> marks the interrupted attempt
    failed and continues from there. One that crashed has its attempts failed with its
    stack dump's first frames as soon as its runner sees it end
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/debug#crashes">Debugging failures</a>). <strong>A run over its
    memory</strong>, in a cgroup of its own, is killed alone and retried with 1.5 times
    the memory (<a href="#resources">Memory and CPU</a>). <strong>A commit that landed before the crash</strong>
    is found by the retry rather than made again. Fuzzing, and a chaos test that kills
    runs, restarts Postgres and races two resumes over many seeds, run in
    <a href={`${ORCA_PRS}/6`} rel="noopener">CI</a>.
  </p>
